1
00:00:00,825 --> 00:00:02,204
Last time, we asked:

2
00:00:02,224 --> 00:00:03,804
A frog and a mosquito!

3
00:00:04,064 --> 00:00:07,484
Both lay their eggs in water, and both have young that look

4
00:00:07,504 --> 00:00:08,944
nothing like the adult.

5
00:00:09,214 --> 00:00:11,064
Are their life cycles the same?

6
00:00:11,111 --> 00:00:12,221
One, yes.

7
00:00:13,441 --> 00:00:14,321
Two, no.

8
00:00:15,799 --> 00:00:17,309
Hold on to your answer!

9
00:00:19,181 --> 00:00:21,861
A little cicada is hiding in this video.

10
00:00:22,111 --> 00:00:23,351
Can you spot it?

11
00:00:23,474 --> 00:00:25,844
Cicadas sing loudly, but they hide well.

12
00:00:26,024 --> 00:00:27,444
Look on the tree trunks!

13
00:00:27,496 --> 00:00:28,956
You against the Buddies!

14
00:00:29,226 --> 00:00:31,076
Lock in first with your fingers.

15
00:00:31,346 --> 00:00:33,176
One finger for every point.

16
00:00:33,757 --> 00:00:35,057
Today you'll learn:

17
00:00:35,262 --> 00:00:36,592
How to compare.

18
00:00:36,788 --> 00:00:38,228
Count the stages.

19
00:00:38,433 --> 00:00:39,663
Look at the young.

20
00:00:40,168 --> 00:00:41,108
Are you ready?

21
00:00:41,548 --> 00:00:42,318
Three!

22
00:00:42,698 --> 00:00:43,358
Two!

23
00:00:43,728 --> 00:00:44,378
One!

24
00:00:44,798 --> 00:00:45,518
Go!

25
00:00:46,907 --> 00:00:47,797
Round one!

26
00:00:48,097 --> 00:00:49,917
Are their life cycles the same?

27
00:00:50,217 --> 00:00:51,527
Show me your fingers!

28
00:00:54,994 --> 00:00:55,974
Locked in!

29
00:00:56,102 --> 00:00:56,822
One!

30
00:00:56,952 --> 00:01:00,432
They both lay eggs in water, and both babies look nothing like

31
00:01:00,452 --> 00:01:01,422
their parents.

32
00:01:01,482 --> 00:01:02,882
So they are the same!

33
00:01:02,932 --> 00:01:03,562
Two!

34
00:01:03,722 --> 00:01:06,682
A frog is big and green, and a mosquito is tiny and

35
00:01:06,702 --> 00:01:07,342
buzzy.

36
00:01:07,572 --> 00:01:09,032
They are totally different!

37
00:01:09,082 --> 00:01:12,382
Two. I counted the stages on our board.

38
00:01:12,602 --> 00:01:15,552
The frog has three, but the mosquito has four.

39
00:01:15,828 --> 00:01:17,938
But I found two things that are the same!

40
00:01:18,018 --> 00:01:20,348
How can their life cycles be different?

41
00:01:20,996 --> 00:01:22,146
Look closer.

42
00:01:22,726 --> 00:01:25,646
When we compare, we look for what is the same, and what

43
00:01:25,666 --> 00:01:26,587
is different.

44
00:01:26,607 --> 00:01:29,127
What is the same are similarities.

45
00:01:29,697 --> 00:01:31,997
What is different are differences.

46
00:01:33,027 --> 00:01:36,527
Similarities are the things both life cycles have in common.

47
00:01:36,757 --> 00:01:39,144
Differences are the ways they are not the same.

48
00:01:39,164 --> 00:01:41,544
Momo found two real similarities.

49
00:01:42,104 --> 00:01:46,004
Both lay their eggs in water, and in both, the young do

50
00:01:46,024 --> 00:01:47,530
not look like the adult.

51
00:01:47,550 --> 00:01:49,150
But is there a difference?

52
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:51,350
Let's count the stages.

53
00:01:53,990 --> 00:01:55,130
Be a raindrop!

54
00:01:55,430 --> 00:01:56,430
Shrink down.

55
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:57,640
Quietly.

56
00:01:59,922 --> 00:02:03,582
You fall into the garden pond, beside some frog eggs.

57
00:02:04,362 --> 00:02:05,582
Days go by.

58
00:02:06,412 --> 00:02:08,412
Out wriggles a tadpole, the young.

59
00:02:08,921 --> 00:02:11,691
It grows legs, and its tail shrinks away.

60
00:02:12,481 --> 00:02:13,861
Out hops a frog.

61
00:02:14,681 --> 00:02:17,071
Egg, young, adult: three stages.

62
00:02:17,594 --> 00:02:18,544
Splash!

63
00:02:19,324 --> 00:02:23,514
You land in a flower-pot plate, beside tiny mosquito eggs.

64
00:02:24,284 --> 00:02:26,364
Out come the wrigglers, the larvae.

65
00:02:26,887 --> 00:02:29,917
Each one curls into a pupa, one more stage!

66
00:02:30,707 --> 00:02:32,557
Then out comes a mosquito.

67
00:02:33,437 --> 00:02:36,517
Egg, larva, pupa, adult: four stages.

68
00:02:38,178 --> 00:02:40,698
The answer is two: no, they are different!

69
00:02:40,978 --> 00:02:42,008
Two fingers?

70
00:02:42,278 --> 00:02:43,444
One finger up!

71
00:02:43,464 --> 00:02:46,844
The frog has three stages, and the mosquito has four.

72
00:02:46,984 --> 00:02:50,079
The mosquito has a pupa, and the frog does not.

73
00:02:50,099 --> 00:02:53,399
Basil picked two, but size does not tell us about a life

74
00:02:53,419 --> 00:02:54,119
cycle.

75
00:02:54,389 --> 00:02:56,164
So only Pip scores a point.

76
00:02:56,184 --> 00:02:58,454
We can see the stages, and count them.

77
00:02:58,744 --> 00:03:01,044
They are observable characteristics.

78
00:03:01,566 --> 00:03:04,186
Observable means we can see it, or count it.

79
00:03:04,336 --> 00:03:06,266
The number of stages is one.

80
00:03:06,486 --> 00:03:08,496
What the young looks like is another!

81
00:03:08,699 --> 00:03:09,939
Say it with me!

82
00:03:10,646 --> 00:03:14,306
The life cycles of the frog and the mosquito are similar because

83
00:03:14,326 --> 00:03:16,526
the young do not look like the adults.

84
00:03:17,016 --> 00:03:20,976
However, the life cycles are different because the frog has three stages

85
00:03:20,996 --> 00:03:24,426
in its life cycle, while the mosquito has four stages in its

86
00:03:24,446 --> 00:03:25,476
life cycle.

87
00:03:28,104 --> 00:03:29,194
My turn!

88
00:03:29,404 --> 00:03:32,494
The frog and the grasshopper both have three stages.

89
00:03:32,684 --> 00:03:35,484
So their life cycles are exactly the same!

90
00:03:35,609 --> 00:03:37,169
Something is not right.

91
00:03:37,439 --> 00:03:39,619
If you can spot it, cross your arms.

92
00:03:39,899 --> 00:03:40,859
Hold on!

93
00:03:46,272 --> 00:03:47,402
Arms crossed?

94
00:03:47,672 --> 00:03:48,882
One finger up!

95
00:03:48,934 --> 00:03:51,764
The number of stages is the same, but you forgot to look

96
00:03:51,784 --> 00:03:52,734
at the young!

97
00:03:53,531 --> 00:03:56,166
Look closer at the young, beside their parents.

98
00:03:56,186 --> 00:03:59,026
The tadpole has a tail and no legs, and it lives in

99
00:03:59,046 --> 00:03:59,686
water.

100
00:03:59,706 --> 00:04:01,427
It looks nothing like a frog!

101
00:04:01,447 --> 00:04:04,367
But the nymph has six legs and two feelers, just like a

102
00:04:04,387 --> 00:04:05,437
grasshopper.

103
00:04:05,687 --> 00:04:07,293
It only has no wings yet!

104
00:04:07,313 --> 00:04:10,383
One thing is the same, and one thing is different.

105
00:04:11,013 --> 00:04:12,443
Always look at both!

106
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:16,440
Say it with me!

107
00:04:17,155 --> 00:04:21,945
When we compare, we can use observable characteristics to identify similarities and

108
00:04:21,965 --> 00:04:24,945
differences between the life cycles of the animals.

109
00:04:27,419 --> 00:04:28,879
Show me your score hand!

110
00:04:29,159 --> 00:04:30,549
One finger or more?

111
00:04:30,869 --> 00:04:32,229
You beat the Buddies.

112
00:04:32,499 --> 00:04:33,529
Two fingers?

113
00:04:33,809 --> 00:04:34,819
High five!

114
00:04:35,391 --> 00:04:36,981
Did you spot the cicada?

115
00:04:37,271 --> 00:04:39,401
It was on the leg of our board's easel!

116
00:04:39,536 --> 00:04:40,846
Here is a fun fact!

117
00:04:40,906 --> 00:04:43,696
Cicadas have three stages, like grasshoppers.

118
00:04:43,716 --> 00:04:48,226
But in America, some cicada nymphs live underground for seventeen years!

119
00:04:48,349 --> 00:04:49,629
Tell someone at home!

120
00:04:50,358 --> 00:04:52,558
Let's read today's key words together!

121
00:04:52,848 --> 00:04:53,608
Ready?

122
00:04:53,945 --> 00:04:54,882
Similarities.

123
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:57,544
Differences.

124
00:04:59,231 --> 00:05:01,121
Observable characteristics.

125
00:05:03,443 --> 00:05:04,383
Well done!

126
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:06,960
Look, the hen is sitting on her eggs!

127
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:09,451
So which came first, the chicken or the egg?

128
00:05:09,471 --> 00:05:11,061
Find out in Part Two!

129
00:05:12,221 --> 00:05:13,322
Say it with us!

130
00:05:14,122 --> 00:05:14,872
Ready?

131
00:05:14,995 --> 00:05:15,655
One!

132
00:05:15,995 --> 00:05:16,695
two!

133
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,175
Now you know!
